## Why this topic matters

{% illo panel="scope" /%}

Data protection is the legal tier you will see most often in Paper 1
scenarios because almost every digital system processes personal data
about somebody. A booking system stores customer names. An HR portal
stores employee records. A school's reporting tool stores pupil grades.
A delivery app holds addresses and order histories. The moment a system
holds information that can be tied back to a living individual, data
protection law applies.

A Paper 1 answer that fails to name data protection in a clear data
protection scenario loses the Name mark, which usually drags the Explain
mark down with it because the rest of the answer is anchored to the
wrong rule. The path through this topic mirrors the Answer Arc: get the
recognition automatic, then build out the rules that earn the Explain
marks.

## What the law actually is

{% illo panel="regime" /%}

The UK's current data protection regime sits on two documents that work
together. The UK General Data Protection Regulation (UK GDPR) is the
substantive rulebook: it sets out the principles, the lawful bases, the
data subject rights and the rules on transfers. The Data Protection Act
2018 (DPA 2018) is the domestic Act that implements UK GDPR and adds
UK-specific provisions, including some additional offences and the
exemptions framework. When you write about "data protection law" in an
exam you can refer to either, but the precise phrasing is "the Data
Protection Act 2018 and the UK GDPR".

The law applies whenever an organisation in the UK is processing
personal data, and to organisations outside the UK that offer goods or
services to people in the UK or monitor their behaviour. "Processing"
is defined very broadly: it covers collecting, storing, using, sharing,
combining and deleting personal data, so almost any operation on the
data counts.

"Personal data" means any information relating to an identified or
identifiable living individual. A name, an email address, an IP address
that can be tied back to a household, a customer reference number that
links to a real account, and a photograph in which someone is
recognisable are all personal data. Truly anonymous statistics (where
no individual can be re-identified) are not personal data and fall
outside the law.

## The roles the law assigns

{% illo panel="actors" /%}

The law gives every organisation handling personal data one of two
roles.

A **data controller** is an organisation that decides why personal data
is being processed and how. The decisions about purpose and means make
it the controller. A retailer that runs its own customer database is
the controller of that data. A school that holds pupil records is the
controller of those records.

A **data processor** processes personal data on behalf of a controller,
following the controller's instructions. A payroll bureau that runs the
salary calculations for a small business is a processor. A cloud
storage provider that hosts customer data for the retailer above is a
processor. Processors carry some legal obligations directly (notably
security and breach notification to the controller) but the controller
remains primarily accountable.

The **data subject** is the individual whose personal data is being
processed. Data subjects have rights, listed below. The data subject is
the person the law is written to protect.

## The seven principles

{% illo panel="principles" /%}

UK GDPR Article 5 sets out the principles that every act of processing
must comply with. Examiners reward students who can name the principle
that is engaged in a scenario, not just say "the data has been mishandled".

1. **Lawfulness, fairness and transparency.** Processing must have one
   of the lawful bases set out in Article 6 (see below), must be fair
   to the data subject, and must be explained to them clearly through
   a privacy notice.

2. **Purpose limitation.** Personal data may only be collected for
   specified, explicit and legitimate purposes and must not be used
   later for a different incompatible purpose. Collecting addresses to
   ship orders and then using them to send unrelated marketing to
   non-customers breaches this principle.

3. **Data minimisation.** Personal data must be adequate, relevant and
   limited to what is necessary for the purpose. Collecting a date of
   birth on a form that only needs to confirm someone is over eighteen
   is a minimisation breach.

4. **Accuracy.** Personal data must be accurate and kept up to date.
   Inaccurate data must be corrected or erased without delay.

5. **Storage limitation.** Personal data must be kept in identifiable
   form for no longer than is necessary. CCTV footage kept indefinitely
   when a thirty-day retention policy would be enough is a storage
   limitation breach.

6. **Integrity and confidentiality.** Personal data must be processed
   in a manner that ensures appropriate security, including protection
   against unauthorised processing, accidental loss, destruction or
   damage. This is the principle engaged by weak passwords, unencrypted
   laptops, unlocked filing cabinets and over-permissive access controls.

7. **Accountability.** The controller is responsible for compliance and
   must be able to demonstrate it through records, policies, training
   and (for high-risk processing) data protection impact assessments.
   Accountability is the wrap-around principle that ties the others
   together.

## The lawful bases

{% illo panel="lawful-bases" /%}

Every act of processing must rest on one of the six lawful bases in
Article 6. Picking the wrong basis, or picking no basis at all, is a
breach of the first principle.

- **Consent.** The data subject has given a clear, specific, informed
  and freely-given indication of agreement through a positive action.
  Pre-ticked boxes are not consent. Consent can be withdrawn at any
  time.

- **Contract.** Processing is necessary to perform a contract with the
  data subject (or to take steps to enter one at the data subject's
  request). Storing a delivery address to ship a customer's order
  rests on this basis.

- **Legal obligation.** Processing is necessary to comply with a legal
  obligation imposed on the controller. Reporting income tax data to
  HMRC rests on this basis.

- **Vital interests.** Processing is necessary to protect the data
  subject's life or someone else's life. Hospitals rely on this in
  emergencies where the patient cannot consent.

- **Public task.** Processing is necessary to perform a task carried
  out in the public interest or in the exercise of official authority.
  Public sector organisations frequently rely on this basis.

- **Legitimate interests.** Processing is necessary for the legitimate
  interests of the controller or a third party, provided those
  interests are not overridden by the rights of the data subject. This
  basis requires a balancing exercise to be documented in advance.

For "special category data" (data revealing racial or ethnic origin,
political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, trade union
membership, health,
sex life, sexual orientation, genetic data or biometric data used for
identification) an additional condition under Article 9 is needed on
top of the Article 6 basis.

## Data subject rights

{% illo panel="rights" /%}

Articles 12 to 22 give data subjects a set of rights they can exercise
against the controller. Each one has scenarios where it commonly bites:

- **Right to be informed.** The controller must give a clear privacy
  notice describing what data is held, why, on what basis, for how
  long, and with whom it is shared.

- **Right of access.** A data subject can submit a subject access
  request and the controller must respond within one month, providing
  a copy of the data and surrounding information.

- **Right to rectification.** Inaccurate personal data must be corrected
  on request.

- **Right to erasure (the "right to be forgotten").** In specified
  circumstances (such as where consent has been withdrawn and there is
  no other lawful basis) the data must be deleted.

- **Right to restrict processing.** The data subject can require the
  controller to stop processing while a dispute is resolved.

- **Right to data portability.** Where processing is based on consent
  or contract and is carried out by automated means, the data subject
  can receive their data in a structured, commonly-used format and
  transmit it to another controller.

- **Right to object.** The data subject can object to processing based
  on legitimate interests or public task, and can object to direct
  marketing at any time. Objections to direct marketing must always be
  honoured.

- **Rights related to automated decision-making.** The data subject has
  the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated
  processing (including profiling) that produces legal or similarly
  significant effects, except in limited circumstances.

## Enforcement and consequences

The Information Commissioner's Office (ICO) is the UK regulator. It
investigates complaints, issues guidance, audits controllers, and where
necessary takes enforcement action. The headline sanction is a
monetary penalty. UK GDPR sets a two-tier maximum: the lower tier (for
example, failure to keep records or to notify a personal data breach)
is up to £8.7 million or 2% of annual global turnover, whichever is
higher. The higher tier (for example, breaching the principles or the
lawful bases, or infringing data subject rights) is up to £17.5 million
or 4% of annual global turnover, whichever is higher.

The DPA 2018 adds criminal offences that sit alongside the regulatory
regime. Section 170 makes it a criminal offence to knowingly or
recklessly obtain, disclose or procure personal data without the
controller's consent. Section 171 covers re-identification of
de-identified data. Section 173 covers altering personal data to
frustrate a subject access request. These offences attach to
individuals (including employees), not just the organisation.

Individuals can also claim compensation in court for material or
non-material damage caused by an infringement.

## What Paper 1 expects

When a Paper 1 question gives you a scenario that involves personal
data, the path through the answer is:

1. **Name the law.** "The Data Protection Act 2018 and the UK GDPR."
   Where the question rewards a more specific Name, also name the
   principle, lawful basis or right that is engaged.

2. **Explain what the relevant rule actually requires.** Quote the
   principle or right in your own words. If the scenario is about a
   weak security arrangement, the rule is the integrity and
   confidentiality principle. If it is about ignoring an opt-out, the
   rule is the right to object to direct marketing (or the withdrawal
   of consent).

3. **Apply the rule to the scenario.** Tie the requirement back to the
   specific facts you were given. The strongest answers quote the
   scenario explicitly: "because the hospital allows any administrator
   to email patient data externally..."

The same path applies to longer extended-response questions, with the
Impact section drawing out consequences for the data subject, the
controller and any wider system (such as public trust in the service).

The worked examples in the Method section, particularly the ones that
demonstrate Name versus Describing and Defining versus Applying, show
this path running on real past-paper questions. Once you have practised
the assessment below, come back to those.
